module test.claim.stage0_partition_closure_live_census_test

import std.types { Bool }
import v2.std.live_tree { LiveTreeDisposition, ReadsLiveTree }
import gunbc.stage0_partition_closure {
  PartitionClosureHolds,
  PartitionClosureRefused,
  partition_closure_verdict,
}

data witness_live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition = ReadsLiveTree

data census_split_note: String = "THE IRREDUCIBLE CENSUS, SPLIT INTO ITS OWN FILE SO ITS SIBLINGS CAN STAY PER-PR. The partition-closure gate has two halves with different costs and different subjects. The MECHANISM half — a missing roster row refuses, the refusal names the incomplete authority, two missing modules produce two defects, and the verdict carries its extent — is decidable over supplied facts, runs on a fixture in 1-4ms, and stays in test.claim.stage0_partition_closure_witness_test where per-PR discovery reaches it. THIS file carries only the CENSUS: does the LIVE roster hold against the LIVE corpus.

WHY IT CANNOT STAY PER-PR. partition_closure_verdict derives dependency_resolution_facts_live and module_declaration_facts_live over pool roots dag and src/v2. Measured on two runs: 173.6s and 177.6s of thread CPU, against the 5s fast-lane budget. CI only ever reported 5040ms, because that is where the budget INTERRUPTS — an interrupt is a lower bound on cost, never the cost, which is why this sat looking like a witness slightly over budget rather than one re-deriving the whole corpus. Its subject IS the corpus breadth, so it cannot be shrunk without measuring less, and narrowing the pool roots would UNDER-APPROXIMATE the edges — the fail-open gunbc.stage0_partition_closure partition_closure_authority_note exists to refuse.

WHY A SEPARATE FILE RATHER THAN A ROW IN THE OLD ONE. The long lane's admission is a file-grain path pattern (gunbc.ci_layer_roots witness_exclusion_frontier), so leaving this witness beside the fixtures would drag all five of them off per-PR discovery with it — deleting the coverage this decomposition exists to create. Exact function-grain admission (gunbc.explicit_witness_admission) would avoid that, but every roster it projects into today is a KNOWN-RED probe roster and this witness is expected to pass, so a green long-lane row has no projection there; wiring one is that carrier's own migration frontier, not this repair. Splitting the file is what lifecycle_survivor_corpus_census_test already does for the same reason, and it needs no new machinery.

THE MARGIN IS THIN AND IS NOT HIDDEN. 177.6s sits about 1.3 percent under the long lane's declared 180000ms ceiling, measured on a contended session container, so this may exceed it on a slower runner. That surfaces as a counted long-lane failure on a 4-hourly cadence rather than a blocked merge, which is why enrolling still beats leaving it on the fast lane — but it is the reason the dissolution names cost reduction and not only self-host."

// The live corpus must HOLD. This is the control that would have caught the three invariant
// candidates tried and discarded during design: roster-completeness (75 emit-plan modules are
// legitimately unassigned), roster-mates-only (`use crate::NonEmptyVec` names a type, not a
// module), and own-pub-mod-only (crates reach each other by glob reexport). Each of those
// would refuse rows that are correct, and each would red exactly here.
//
// It is also the paired direction for the fixture REDs next door: a mechanism that never
// refuses fails those, and one that always refuses fails this. Neither file is sufficient
// alone, which is why the split moves the census and keeps the pairing.
test fn w_live_corpus_partition_closure_holds() -> Bool {
  match partition_closure_verdict() {
    PartitionClosureHolds { extent: _ } => true
    PartitionClosureRefused { extent: _, defects: _ } => false
  }
}
